From 67f4341eeadc3b5d19d6ebb86d81b3c026babde8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian Bockelman <bbockelman@morgridge.org>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 07:48:58 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Add a development container and CI, and repair the test
 suite

The test suite did not build, so there was no green baseline to work from and
no way to tell whether a change broke anything.  This gets one running, in a
reproducible environment, on every push and pull request.

Config had no constructor, so the bool and int members of a Config that is
never load()ed held indeterminate values.  Config::load() applies defaults for
all of them, but the unit tests construct a Config directly and read
http_basic_auth from it, which made their behaviour depend on the stack.  They
are now initialized in-class to the values load() applies.

test_pam_oauth2_device had not been updated for the pam_oauth2_curl and
pam_oauth2_log refactoring: it called the pre-refactoring signatures and
default-constructed a pam_oauth2_log, which no longer has a default
constructor.  pam_oauth2_device.hpp used Config in its declarations without
including config.hpp, and only compiled because the .cpp included it first.
The tests also passed credentials only as function arguments, where
pam_oauth2_curl takes them from the Config, so the module sent "Basic Og==" --
an empty user and password -- and mock_server rejected it; they now populate
the Config the way pam_sm_authenticate does.  mock_server accepts credentials
by HTTP Basic auth or in the request body, as RFC 6749 section 2.3.1 permits,
rather than only the latter.  Two ConfigTest cases expected a json::parse_error
where read_file() now throws std::runtime_error, and a usermap from before the
*bypass* entry was added.

test_pam_oauth2_device also needs mock_server.py listening on port 8042, which
had to be started by hand, so `make test` failed unless you had read
test/README.md first.  test/run_tests.sh now starts it, waits for it to accept
connections, runs the binaries and shuts it down.

.devcontainer builds on AlmaLinux 9, close to the platforms the module is
packaged for and overridable through IMAGE_BASE, carrying the compiler, the
PAM/curl/LDAP headers, GoogleTest, pamtester, gdb and valgrind, and running as
an unprivileged uid 1000 so a bind-mounted checkout stays writable.

CI builds and tests on AlmaLinux 8 and 9 and separately builds the dev
container and runs the same suite inside it, so the two cannot drift apart.
EL8 needs two workarounds -- its image ships a GPG key older than the metadata
its repositories are signed with, and gtest-devel is in PowerTools there rather
than EPEL -- and both must be applied only on EL8: the key is SHA1-signed and
EL9's crypto policy rejects it, failing the step outright.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
